WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.319
No matter the movie, Chris Evans always seems
to be having a good time — something made

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.760
clear by the outtakes, behind-the-scenes footage,
and bloopers he's left behind.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.800
Here are some times when Evans just couldn't
keep it together while shooting a scene.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.420
Edgar Wright's Scott Pilgrim vs. the World
is a triumph of comedy and sci-fi, and the

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.680
whole thing feels like a video game without
coming across as campy.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.960
To be with his dream girl, Ramona Flowers,
Scott must defeat her seven evil exes, including

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.050
Lucas Lee, a former skateboarder turned gravelly-voiced
macho movie actor.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.460
Wright enlisted actual action star Chris Evans
to play this fictional action star, although

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.370
Evans is probably not as aggressive, frightening,
and toxic as Lucas.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.850
Take for example this moment on set, when
Evans is supposed to be threatening Scott

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.160
and instilling deep fear in the underdog.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.110
That's kind of hard to do when you keep losing
your train of thought and forgetting your

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:45.620
dialogue.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.400
“You are a pretty good actor."

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.040
"I'm more than pretty good.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.950
That's not the line!”

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.300
“I’m not that good.”

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:57.940
Long before Chris Evans and Scarlett Johansson
joined the Avengers, they'd already worked

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:03.809
together twice: In the 2004 college heist
film The Perfect Score, and the 2007 adaptation

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:05.960
of the bestselling novel The Nanny Diaries.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.150
The future Captain America played Hayden,
an upper-crust, Upper East Side "Harvard hottie"

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.030
love interest for Johansson's nanny.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.920
The character might be cool and calm, but
Evans certainly isn't.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.450
He has a tough time with wordy mouthfuls of
dialogue.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.100
"And as soon as you get any closer to that
building, you won't be (gibberish)."

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.180
He wound up blaming his troubles on loud noises
outside of the camera's view, as well as the

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.430
fact that there were half a dozen people running
straight at him.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.580
Solid excuses.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.920
Two most powerful objects in comic book lore
have to be Mjolnir, Thor's mighty hammer,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.420
and Captain America's nigh-invulnerable shield.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.460
As seen throughout hundreds of comic books
and almost as many Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.420
movies, Cap's shield gives Mjolnir a run for
its money.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.420
Its unique steel and Vibranium composition
allows it to absorb impact and energy alike.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.750
If Captain America ducks behind it, under
it, or over it, he can keep himself safe from

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.700
pretty much anything in the universe.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.890
This blooper captured on the set of Captain
America: The Winter Soldier shockingly revealed

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.680
the only thing so powerful that not even this
impeccable weapon and piece of protective

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.340
gear can make it bend to its will: a door.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.500
That shield is just a prop, and it doesn't
give Evans the oomph he needs to bust down

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.060
the big door.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.360
Chris Evans just can't seem to get a break,
props-wise.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.540
Did he accidentally offend someone in the
Marvel Studios prop department?

00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:26.330
Did he skip "acting with props day" in drama
school?

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:30.360
For whatever reason, there are even more bloopers
involving props not cooperating with the guy

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.230
who just wants to play Captain America with
proper gravitas.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.860
To his credit, Evans gives it his all.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.660
“We need a plan of attack.”

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.740
This backpack is the greatest villain Captain
America has ever faced or will ever face onscreen,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.440
it seems.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.540
It wriggles its way out of Evans' control
over and over again.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.200
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, Evans' Captain
America makes a cameo appearance, starring

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.560
in a short educational film that Peter Parker
watches in school.

00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:05.360
It's an arrangement that makes sense — if
any Avenger is going to film a public service

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.340
announcement to remind all the boys and girls
out there to eat a nutritious, balanced lunch,

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.260
it's Captain America.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.280
He's a product of the 1940s, when such aw-shucks,
gee-whiz kinds of PSAs from movie serial and

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.730
radio stars were the norm.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.200
Of course, this is all so ridiculous that
not even Evans can get through it with a straight

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:21.760
face.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.280
“Hi, I'm Captain America.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.360
Whether you're a student, or a soldier, there's
one thing that will always give you an edge:

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.020
A hot lunch."

00:03:32.029 --> 00:03:35.260
It's a super-dramatic, pivotal moment in Avengers:
Age of Ultron.

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:39.409
In a commanding, "everybody pay attention"
tone, and with cohort Black Widow at his side,

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:44.329
a suited-up, ready for battle Captain America
boldly intones, "Avengers…" but then stops.

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:46.980
It's like he's waiting for a dramatic musical
stinger.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.300
The audience is waiting for an "Assemble"
to finish the catch phrase.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:50.860
But it never comes.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.640
"Avengers…

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:56.860
Ehhhh?"

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.520
It would seem Evans was pausing for effect
before delivering the classic line.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.120
He was probably just messing around for fun,
because he quickly turns the moment into a

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:04.660
goof.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.880
And the movie itself cuts to the credits before
the line finishes.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.340
Acting isn't the most physically taxing or
mentally draining job in the world, but it's

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.180
not without its challenges, particularly when
the job is acting in a superhero movie.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.220
Playing Captain America involves a lot of
physicality for Evans.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.900
He's had to do one-on-one fight scenes and
tightly choreographed group fight scenes,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.900
pretended to operate vehicles, dangled from
wires, and taken on villains in front of a

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:27.900
green screen.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.160
He also has to run a lot.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.580
Captain America is always running to dole
out good old fashioned American justice to

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.080
bad guys.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.000
In this take for a pulse-pounding scene for
Captain America: The Winter Soldier, Cap and

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.420
Falcon quickly hoof it up to where they need
to be, but in the wrong direction.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:41.940
“How do we know the good guys from the bad
guys?

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.360
We're supposed to be running…"

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.360
This Avengers moment is all about sheer chaos:
A climactic, apocalyptic battle rages, resulting

00:04:53.379 --> 00:04:54.789
in death and destruction.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:57.700
These moments are among the toughest for a
film crew to commit to celluloid, as there

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.600
are so many moving parts involved in getting
it just right.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.380
That also means the chance for something to
go wrong is much higher than it is for a simple,

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:05.880
straightforward scene.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.200
In this instance, one Chris or the other is
the thing that goes wrong.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:13.720
His Captain America lays on the ground, a
leg awkwardly hanging in the air above him,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.760
awaiting a cue from co-star Chris Hemsworth.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:21.960
"Oh, is Thor comin' in?"

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.900
But since Evans is the one laying there in
the foreground of the shot, he's the one who

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:27.480
has to take one for the team.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.040
And he does it with a laugh.

